---
import DocLayout from '~/layouts/DocLayout.astro';
import { PROVIDER_COUNT } from '~/data/site';
import { COMPARE_CHECKED, PAGE_VIEW, compareTable } from '~/data/compare';

const path = '/cert-manager-alternative';
const title = 'CertMate vs cert-manager, Caddy, Traefik & certbot';
const description =
  'CertMate compared with cert-manager, Caddy, Traefik and certbot: what each does best, and when a standalone certificate manager with a UI and API fits.';

// The cells live in src/data/compare.ts, which the home's table reads too.
const table = compareTable(PAGE_VIEW, 'en');
---

<DocLayout lang="en" path={path} title={title} description={description} product>
  <h1>CertMate vs cert-manager, Caddy, Traefik &amp; certbot</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    These tools all "do TLS", but they solve different problems. Here's an
    honest take on where each one fits — and where CertMate is (and isn't) the
    right choice.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-8 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="border-b border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left dark:border-gray-600"></th>
          {table.columns.map((c, i) => (
            <th class={`border-b border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold dark:border-gray-600 ${i === 0 ? 'text-primary' : 'text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200'}`}>{c}</th>
          ))}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {table.rows.map((r) => (
          <tr class="align-top">
            <td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 font-medium text-gray-700 dark:border-gray-800 dark:text-gray-300">{r.label}</td>
            {r.cells.map((cell, i) => (
              <td class={`border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 dark:border-gray-800 ${i === 0 ? 'font-medium text-gray-900 dark:text-white' : 'text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400'}`}>{cell}</td>
            ))}
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="not-prose -mt-4 mb-8 text-xs text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">{COMPARE_CHECKED.en}</p>

  <h2>When each tool is the right call</h2>

  <h3>Use cert-manager if you live in Kubernetes</h3>
  <p>
    cert-manager is the de-facto standard for in-cluster certificate automation.
    If every workload that needs a certificate is a Kubernetes <code>Ingress</code>
    or <code>Gateway</code>, cert-manager is hard to beat. It is, by design,
    Kubernetes-only — it doesn't help with the VM running your load balancer, the
    bare-metal mail server, or the appliance that just needs a PEM file dropped on
    disk.
  </p>

  <h3>Use Caddy or Traefik if they <em>are</em> your edge</h3>
  <p>
    Caddy and Traefik get automatic HTTPS effectively for free for the traffic
    they terminate. That's excellent — if Caddy or Traefik is the server in front
    of your app. They're not built to be a central place to issue, track and push
    certificates to <em>other</em> systems you don't proxy.
  </p>

  <h3>Use certbot for a single host</h3>
  <p>
    certbot is the reference ACME client and perfect for one or a few hosts driven
    from cron. As the host count grows, you end up writing the orchestration,
    inventory, access control and deployment glue yourself — which is exactly the
    layer the tools above (and CertMate) provide.
  </p>

  <h2>Where CertMate fits</h2>
  <p>
    CertMate is a <strong>standalone certificate manager with a web UI and a REST
    API</strong>. It's the right fit when you need to issue, renew and
    <em>deploy</em> certificates across many hosts, providers and CAs — inside or
    alongside Kubernetes, on VMs, bare metal or appliances — with role-based access
    and an API your automation can call. It speaks {PROVIDER_COUNT} DNS providers,
    multiple CAs (Let's Encrypt, ZeroSSL, Google Trust Services, DigiCert, SSL.com,
    Sectigo, Actalis, and a built-in private CA), and ships deploy hooks so a renewed certificate is
    actually pushed to the systems that serve it.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is <strong>not</strong> a Kubernetes-native controller (use cert-manager
    in-cluster), and it doesn't replace your web server's built-in HTTPS if Caddy
    or Traefik already covers everything you run. Many teams use CertMate
    <em>alongside</em> those — as the central, audited control plane for the certs
    that live outside the cluster.
  </p>
</DocLayout>
